<title>DENMARK: Morgan Stanley sees lower Danish 1997 inflation.</title>
<headline>Morgan Stanley sees lower Danish 1997 inflation.</headline>
<dateline>COPENHAGEN 1997-07-09</dateline>
<text>
<p>In its latest survey U.S. investment bank Morgan Stanley on Wednesday lowered its forecast for Denmark's 1997 inflation to 2.0 percent from 2.1 percent in its last forecast in June.</p>
<p>At the same time it left its estimate for GDP (gross domestic product) growth unchanged at 3.5 percent and forecast a 1997 current account surplus of 11.5 billion crowns.</p>
<p>The latest Danish Economy Ministry forecast in May foresaw 3.3 percent GDP growth, 2.3 percent inflation, 7.8 percent unemployment and a current account surplus of 12.0 billion crowns for this year.</p>
<p>The following estimates were made by 10 leading local and international organisations in recent months.</p>
<p>	  (pct)	 GDP    inflation  unemployment c/a (bln Dkk)</p>
<p>Morgan Stanley (7/97) 3.5  2.0	  8.0	   11.5</p>
<p>Nykredit (7/97)     2.6    2.1	  8.2	   10.0</p>
<p>Jyske Bank (6/97)   3.2    2.2	  7.9	   10.0</p>
<p>JP Morgan (6/97)    3.4    2.2	  7.8	   10.7</p>
<p>Barclays  (6/97)    3.0    2.3	  7.8	    9.8</p>
<p>AE-Council(6/97)    3.3    2.3	  7.8	   11.0</p>
<p>M Stanley (6/97)    3.5    2.1	  8.0	    --</p>
<p>Unidanmark (6/97)   3.2    2.3	  7.9	   11.0</p>
<p>PaineWebber (6/97)  2.8    2.1	  7.8	   18.1</p>
<p>Danske Bank (6/97)  3.1    2.2	  7.9	   14.8</p>
<p>--Chris Follett, Copenhagen newsroom +45-33969652</p>
